{"maxId":338,"entries":{"chapter-1-study-biology-of-aging-and-longevity|Chapter 1 Study: Biology of Aging and Longevity":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Aging predisposes to multiple diseases and loss of homeostasis; delaying a single illness is not equivalent to slowing the aging process (p3-4).":["u3"],"copy|Dietary and single-gene manipulations can postpone multiple age-associated disorders together in animals (p4-5).":["u4"],"copy|Rodents fed 30–40% less typically live up to 40% longer; benefits depend on diet composition, timing and model (p5).":["u5"],"copy|Dietary restriction improves human metabolic risk measures, but whether restriction without malnutrition extends human longevity is unknown (p6).":["u6"],"copy|Insulin/IGF signaling and FoxO connect nutrient availability with stress resistance; effects vary by tissue and organism (p7-8).":["u7"],"copy|TOR regulates growth, translation and protein quality control; autophagy removes damaged material (p8-9).":["u8"],"copy|Sirtuins link NAD availability to protein modification and metabolism; mammalian family members have distinct effects (p9-10).":["u9"],"copy|Stress resistance, genome maintenance and proteostasis overlap; antioxidant treatment does not reliably extend lifespan in model organisms (p10-12).":["u10"],"copy|Telomere loss can limit cell replication; senescence intersects with tumor suppression, tissue dysfunction and cancer. Cell-culture senescence is not the whole aging process (p13-14).":["u11"],"copy|Mouse longevity effects may depend on sex, genetic background and laboratory conditions (p14-16).":["u12"],"copy|Rapamycin, canagliflozin, acarbose and estradiol-related interventions extend mouse survival in the studies discussed; these are experimental longevity findings, not human dosing recommendations (p17).":["u13"],"copy|Genetic factors account for about 15–20% of human lifespan variation; disease susceptibility and longevity are difficult to separate (p17-19).":["u14"],"copy|APOE associations replicate more consistently than many candidate longevity loci; a healthy-aging phenotype is more informative than age at death alone (p19).":["u15"],"copy|Werner and Hutchinson–Gilford syndromes mimic selected aging features but also differ substantially from normal aging (p20).":["u16"],"copy|The book finds no evidence that marketed supplements such as melatonin, DHEA or resveratrol retard human aging; the research goal is longer healthy life, not merely prolonged disability (p21-22).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 1s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 1; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Aging is modifiable in model organisms":["u21"],"copy|Interacting biological pathways":["u22"],"copy|Translation and its limits":["u23"],"copy|Past-paper questions linked to this chapter appear in the Exam questions tab.":["u24"],"copy|CHAPTER 1 / HAZZARD":["u19"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u25"],"copy|Which macronutrient restriction most often lengthened lifespan in animal experiments? June 2022 Q51.":["u26"],"copy|Which of four genes was absent from the book’s list of candidate longevity associations? June 2022 Q71.":["u27"],"dietary-restriction-and-the-meaning-of-longer-survival|Dietary restriction and the meaning of longer survival":["u28"],"genes-associated-with-human-longevity|Genes associated with human longevity":["u29"],"how-the-experimental-findings-fit-together|How the experimental findings fit together":["u30"],"copy|Nutrient signals alter growth and cell maintenance. Insulin and IGF signaling affect FoxO transcription factors, while TOR regulates protein translation. Inhibition of TOR by rapamycin can extend survival in experimental animals. This supports a role for nutrient sensing in aging. It does not establish a human longevity treatment (p7–9, p17).":["u31"],"copy|Sirtuins use NAD to remove chemical groups from protein substrates. Their effects vary among family members and tissues. A change in metabolism or stress resistance is a possible link to longevity, and an isolated pathway response is insufficient to demonstrate longer healthy life (p9–12).":["u32"],"copy|A senescent cell stops dividing and can persist in tissue, secreting cytokines and enzymes that affect its surroundings. Telomere shortening can limit repeated division. Neither finding alone explains aging in tissues with few dividing cells (p13–14).":["u33"],"copy|Benefits observed in a particular mouse strain or sex may differ in another population. Progeroid syndromes reproduce selected features of aging, and a treatment that improves a single human outcome still needs evidence for any broader claim about healthy lifespan (p14–22).":["u34"],"israeli-context|Israeli context — אריכות ימים ותפקוד בישראל":["u35"],"choosing-the-right-comparison-in-longevity-studies|Choosing the right comparison in longevity studies":["u323"],"copy|Separate what an intervention changes from what was measured. Rodents given less food can outlive freely fed controls, but a lower-protein diet can also prolong life when total calorie intake is similar. In methionine-restricted rats the reported increase was 40% versus a standard diet; matching the calories without restricting methionine produced a much smaller effect (p5–7).":["u324"],"copy|Rhesus monkey studies described delays in indices of aging and age-related disease, but lifespan results differed between studies. Proposed explanations include the control diet. A change in a human metabolic risk marker is further removed from demonstrating a change in human lifespan (p6).":["u325"],"copy|A longevity allele can improve survival by reducing one cause of death. To distinguish this from broadly slower aging, look for preserved function or delayed pathology across several tissues and diseases, and check whether the association replicates in different populations (p17–19).":["u326"],"what-would-count-as-slowing-aging|What would count as slowing aging?":["u327"],"why-diet-composition-changes-the-interpretation-of-a-feeding-study|Why diet composition changes the interpretation of a feeding study":["u328"],"from-growth-signals-to-maintenance-of-the-cell|From growth signals to maintenance of the cell":["u329"],"damage-resilience-and-the-limits-of-single-mechanism-explanations|Damage, resilience and the limits of single-mechanism explanations":["u330"],"mouse-populations-drug-studies-and-limits-to-extrapolation|Mouse populations, drug studies and limits to extrapolation":["u331"],"human-genetics-accelerated-aging-syndromes-and-preventive-medicine|Human genetics, accelerated-aging syndromes and preventive medicine":["u332"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u333"],"copy|Do not turn the relative protein content of a long-lived animal cohort into a human dietary prescription (p6–7).":["u334"],"copy|A candidate-gene association in centenarians and an independently replicated genome-wide result are different levels of evidence (p19).":["u335"],"copy|Senescence in cultured dividing cells models only part of the biology of an aging organism (p13–14).":["u336"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u337"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u338"],"copy|June 2022 Q51 tested the balance of macronutrients.":["u36"],"copy|Among diets compared in animal studies, relatively little protein compared with the other macronutrients commonly produced the longest lifespan.":["u37"],"copy|The total calorie count alone could not account for the result.":["u38"],"copy|Restricting methionine, an essential amino acid, also extended survival in rodents even when they consumed more calories per unit of lean mass than controls (p6–7).":["u39"],"copy|Survival versus aging.":["u40"],"copy|Preventing one fatal illness can lengthen life.":["u41"],"copy|The stronger observation in restricted rodents was that changes in renewing tissues, nondividing neurons and extracellular tissues were delayed together.":["u42"],"copy|That breadth supports a shared aging process (p4–5).":["u43"],"copy|What can be said to a patient?":["u44"],"copy|In humans, dietary restriction has improved measures such as blood pressure and lipids.":["u45"],"copy|Those outcomes can be measured long before lifespan.":["u46"],"copy|The observations do not establish that restriction without malnutrition prolongs human life or that an older patient should restrict protein for longevity (p6).":["u47"],"copy|June 2022 Q71 asked for the exception among four genes.":["u48"],"copy|The candidate-gene studies cited in the chapter found variants near FOXO3A, APOE and SIRT3 more often in long-lived people.":["u49"],"copy|KITLG is absent from that list.":["u50"],"copy|Enrichment in a selected group does not establish causality or predict how long an individual will live (p19).":["u51"],"copy|Candidate association versus replication.":["u52"],"copy|FOXO3A participates in insulin and IGF signaling, SIRT3 regulates mitochondrial processes, and APOE variants influence cardiovascular and Alzheimer disease risk.":["u53"],"copy|In the genome-wide screens reviewed in this chapter, only the APOE association had appeared in multiple studies.":["u54"],"copy|A variant may influence age at death by altering susceptibility to a particular disease.":["u55"],"copy|This is why the chapter discusses healthy aging as an outcome in addition to survival (p17–19).":["u56"],"copy|כמה אנשים מגיעים לגיל מבוגר?":["u57"],"copy|בשנת 2023 חיו בישראל 1,254,200 בני 65 ומעלה.":["u58"],"copy|מספרם גדל באותה שנה ב־52,400.":["u59"],"copy|אלה נתונים על גודל קבוצת הגיל באוכלוסייה.":["u60"],"copy|מקור: מכון מאיירס־ג׳וינט־ברוקדייל, שנתון 2025, לקט נתונים אינפוגרפי, עמ׳ 34, נתוני 2023.":["u61"],"copy|תוחלת חיים.":["u62"],"copy|בתרשים הישראלי לפי מין, תוחלת החיים המוצגת לשנת 2023 היא כ־82 שנים לגברים וכ־86 שנים לנשים.":["u63"],"copy|בשנת 1975 הערכים המקבילים היו כ־70 וכ־74 שנים.":["u64"],"copy|אלה ערכים מעוגלים מלידה.":["u65"],"copy|מקור: ברוקדייל, לקט נתונים אינפוגרפי 2025, עמ׳ 35, התרשים לשנים 1975–2023.":["u66"],"copy|שנים בחיים ותפקוד בחיי היום־יום.":["u67"],"copy|בממוצע לשנים 2022–2024 דיווחו 21% מבני 65–74 על קושי בהליכה או בעלייה במדרגות, לעומת 37% מבני 75 ומעלה.":["u68"],"copy|השיעורים כוללים גם את מי שאינם מסוגלים לבצע את הפעולה כלל.":["u69"],"copy|מקור: ברוקדייל, לקט נתונים אינפוגרפי 2025, עמ׳ 36, התרשים על קשיי תפקוד.":["u70"],"copy|A geroscience claim usually contains two separate propositions: an animal or person lives longer, and aging itself has slowed.":["u133"],"copy|A treatment that prevents one common fatal illness can satisfy the first proposition without satisfying the second.":["u134"],"copy|Look for changes in the timing of several independent late-life conditions or in function across unrelated tissues.":["u135"],"copy|If renal pathology, connective-tissue changes, immune responses and cognitive decline are all delayed, a shared change in susceptibility is more plausible than an isolated treatment of one disease.":["u136"],"copy|Conversely, a longer-lived group that merely avoids one particular tumor still provides interesting evidence about that tumor (p3–5).":["u137"],"copy|Age-related changes are coordinated without necessarily being dictated by a single developmental clock.":["u138"],"copy|Species differ markedly in the period over which their organs remain functional.":["u139"],"copy|A timing of damage that is tolerable in a short-lived species would be a serious problem in a long-lived one.":["u140"],"copy|The evolutionary question is how long investment in maintenance and defense pays off under the conditions a species ordinarily faces.":["u141"],"copy|Coordinated timing of multiple age changes can arise from how resources are allocated and damage is handled, without requiring a program whose purpose is to cause each lesion on schedule (p3–5).":["u142"],"copy|Mean or median lifespan and survival at the late end of the distribution are also different outcomes.":["u143"],"copy|Preventing early deaths can raise the average without substantially changing the oldest ages attained.":["u144"],"copy|A survival curve whose separation persists later in life gives a different signal, although the causes of death and the phenotype of survivors still matter.":["u145"],"copy|Compare animals with suitable controls across the life course and examine whether multiple forms of age-dependent pathology shift with survival.":["u146"],"copy|Measuring a molecular marker in a young animal is several inferential steps short of that demonstration (p4–5, p16–18).":["u147"],"copy|A freely fed control group differs from a restricted group in more than total energy intake.":["u148"],"copy|Amounts and relative proportions of protein, carbohydrate and other nutrients can change, as can the pattern of metabolic signaling.":["u149"],"copy|Figure 1-1 illustrates the issue with a dietary response surface in flies.":["u150"],"copy|Lifespan varies across nutrient mixtures, so two diets with similar caloric value need not produce identical survival.":["u151"],"copy|Follow the direction of the protein-to-other-macronutrient comparison rather than choosing a nutrient solely because it provides the most calories per gram (p5–7).":["u152"],"copy|In the rodent methionine example the salient comparison is restriction of a particular essential amino acid against a standard diet and against a caloric comparison.":["u153"],"copy|The longevity effect was substantially greater than would be expected from the small reduction attributable to matching calories.":["u154"],"copy|This supports a role for nutrient-specific signals as well as energy balance.":["u155"],"copy|It does not mean that deficiency of any arbitrarily chosen amino acid has the same effect, or that the result can be transferred unchanged from a laboratory diet to an older person's meals (p6–7).":["u156"],"copy|The rhesus monkey studies are informative partly because they did not give identical survival results.":["u157"],"copy|Both reported changes in age-related physiology or pathology, but the control diets and other conditions differed.":["u158"],"copy|When a restricted group is compared with animals consuming an unusually rich diet, part of the apparent advantage may reflect avoiding harm in the control group.":["u159"],"copy|In another study, a comparatively healthy control diet can reduce that contrast.":["u160"],"copy|An intervention can improve clinically relevant risk factors yet still lack evidence about long-term human survival (p5–6).":["u161"],"copy|Figure 1-2 uses findings from simple animals to connect nutrient and hormonal signals with a change in gene regulation.":["u162"],"copy|Insulin-like signaling can influence whether FoxO-family transcription factors promote programs associated with stress resistance and maintenance.":["u163"],"copy|The important principle is the downstream change in many activities rather than an assumption that a single small molecule is “the aging hormone.”":["u164"],"copy|The location and timing of a signal matter.":["u165"],"copy|A result seen after a lifelong change in a worm is a reason to ask a question in mammals, not evidence about what a clinician should prescribe to an older adult (p7–8).":["u166"],"copy|Figure 1-3 provides a mammalian example.":["u167"],"copy|Snell dwarf mice are approximately one-third the size of normal littermates, have an approximately 40% longer lifespan in the displayed comparison, and show less severe kidney pathology late in life.":["u168"],"copy|The survival curve and the tissue finding answer different questions: one shows how long animals lived, the other shows that a nonfatal age-related process was also altered.":["u169"],"copy|Small size is a visible feature of these mice, but one cannot conclude from this experiment alone that simply making an adult animal smaller retards aging (p8–9).":["u170"],"copy|Figure 1-4 asks whether early-life experience can influence late-life outcomes even after the early exposure has ended.":["u171"],"copy|In the dwarf-mouse comparison, early growth hormone exposure shifted survival toward that of controls.":["u172"],"copy|In the complementary experiment, temporary differences in early nutritional availability were followed by different survival patterns much later.":["u173"],"copy|Together they support lasting effects of developmental nutrition and hormones.":["u174"],"copy|They do not establish that the same hormonal manipulation late in life would have the same effect or that altering a child's growth would be a human longevity intervention (p9–10).":["u175"],"copy|Nutrient-sensitive TOR signaling links growth to protein synthesis and cellular housekeeping.":["u176"],"copy|Protein quality depends on producing the right proteins, folding them, removing damaged or misfolded material and recycling components.":["u177"],"copy|Autophagy contributes to the last two tasks.":["u178"],"copy|Changing the emphasis from continued synthesis toward maintenance may affect multiple tissues and therefore several aging phenotypes at once.":["u179"],"copy|Figure 1-5 connects this model to insulin–IGF signaling.":["u180"],"copy|The pathways overlap, so a survival change attributed to TOR need not imply that every benefit of dietary restriction is mediated through TOR alone (p8–11).":["u181"],"copy|Sirtuins are another proposed link between nutrient availability and the handling of proteins.":["u182"],"copy|Their dependence on NAD connects their activity to cellular metabolism, but individual family members have different substrates, locations and functions.":["u183"],"copy|An effect on a mitochondrial process and an effect on nuclear gene regulation should therefore not be collapsed into one generic sirtuin effect.":["u184"],"copy|The chapter discusses findings that generated enthusiasm for this family alongside uncertainties about which effects actually extend healthy life.":["u185"],"copy|Demonstrating altered enzyme activity is easier than demonstrating a delay of multiple age-related diseases (p9–12).":["u186"],"copy|Figure 1-6 relates exceptional longevity to resistance of cells obtained from long-lived animals or species to selected stresses.":["u187"],"copy|It includes the amount of an oxidative stressor associated with death of half the cells, along with changes in oxidized proteins.":["u188"],"copy|The outcome is resistance in an experimental cellular comparison.":["u189"],"copy|It does not directly measure organ function or remaining lifespan of the animal.":["u190"],"copy|The broader inference is that defenses against several kinds of damage might contribute to longevity, particularly if stress resistance tracks delayed pathology in the intact organism (p10–12).":["u191"],"copy|The oxidative-damage hypothesis makes an appealing prediction: if reactive oxygen species cause much age-associated injury, increasing antioxidant defenses should reliably prolong life.":["u192"],"copy|The results across model organisms and antioxidant interventions do not support such a simple rule.":["u193"],"copy|Reactive molecules take part in signaling as well as damage, and defenses can differ across tissues.":["u194"],"copy|Evidence that a particular oxidized product accumulates with age does not identify it as the rate-limiting cause of aging.":["u195"],"copy|Similarly, a supplement that changes a laboratory oxidation measure has not thereby shown a survival or functional benefit (p11–13, p21).":["u196"],"copy|Genome maintenance raises the same causal question from another direction.":["u197"],"copy|Accumulated lesions, imperfect repair and mutations could contribute to disease or cellular dysfunction over time.":["u198"],"copy|Conditions with unusually severe defects can identify functions important for tissue integrity.":["u199"],"copy|Yet producing a striking defect early in life is not necessarily a model of the ordinary rate of deterioration in all tissues.":["u200"],"copy|Examine whether the model reproduces the pattern, timing and distribution of typical age changes, rather than treating any shortened survival as “accelerated aging” (p12–13, p20).":["u201"],"copy|Figure 1-7 shows two paths from critically shortened telomeres.":["u202"],"copy|With an intact cell-cycle response, an uncapped chromosome end can be recognized as damage and division can arrest.":["u203"],"copy|Checkpoint failure can instead permit chromosomal instability and contribute to malignant transformation.":["u204"],"copy|Arrest can protect against expansion of a potentially dangerous cell while limiting the renewal of a tissue.":["u205"],"copy|A senescent cell can persist and release factors that affect nearby cells.":["u206"],"copy|These mechanisms explain why the same process can have both protective and adverse consequences, and why simply lengthening telomeres is not an uncomplicated solution (p13–14).":["u207"],"copy|Replicative limits are easiest to demonstrate in dividing cells.":["u208"],"copy|They cannot alone explain coordinated change in neurons that rarely divide or the mechanical properties of extracellular collagen.":["u209"],"copy|The abundance and biological effect of senescent cells in a tissue also need to be distinguished.":["u210"],"copy|A small population that alters its environment need not account for most cells to be relevant, but measuring an isolated marker is not proof of a causal effect on the whole organism.":["u211"],"copy|Ask which clinical phenotype follows and whether interventions alter more than the chosen marker (p4–5, p13–14).":["u212"],"copy|Different mouse genotypes can lead to longer survival through different combinations of growth, endocrine function, metabolism and disease susceptibility.":["u213"],"copy|An effect confined to a particular strain or sex may be real in that group while having limited generality.":["u214"],"copy|Independent replication and attention to controls, housing, diet, competing illness and age at treatment are therefore important when comparing reports.":["u215"],"copy|A change that corrects one unusually prevalent disease in a particular strain has a different implication from a change that repeatedly postpones multiple late-life phenotypes across a genetically diverse population (p14–17).":["u216"],"copy|Figure 1-8 compares body size, circulating IGF-1 and longevity across dog breeds.":["u217"],"copy|Its message concerns variation within a species: large and small breeds differ in their growth and lifespan patterns.":["u218"],"copy|Table 1-1 turns to associations in humans and illustrates why stature cannot be presented as a universal predictor of better survival.":["u219"],"copy|In selected populations shorter stature was associated with a lower risk of some neoplastic diseases, while associations for cardiovascular and other outcomes could point the other way.":["u220"],"copy|The competing disease risks make an all-cause conclusion more complicated than the direction for one cancer (p14–16).":["u221"],"copy|Figure 1-9 combines survival curves with pathology and an extracellular tissue measurement.":["u222"],"copy|In the mouse experiments shown, rapamycin was delivered in food at 4.7, 14 or 42 mg per kg of food, and acarbose in another panel at 1,000 mg per kg of food.":["u223"],"copy|These are concentrations in laboratory chow, not body-weight doses or prescriptions.":["u224"],"copy|The rapamycin comparisons include less frequent late-life pathology and delayed changes in tendon collagen elasticity.":["u225"],"copy|This supports broader inquiry than a survival curve alone and still leaves efficacy, adverse effects and suitability for human prevention unresolved (p17–18).":["u226"],"copy|Read the other drug comparisons with sex and endpoint attached.":["u227"],"copy|The chapter reports rapamycin extending mouse lifespan in both sexes, with increases as high as 26% at the highest dose discussed.":["u228"],"copy|Acarbose increased male lifespan by 22%, with a smaller significant effect in females.":["u229"],"copy|Canagliflozin extended median lifespan in males by 14%, and 17-alpha-estradiol extended male lifespan by 19%; neither extended female lifespan in the studies summarized.":["u230"],"copy|Figure 1-10 places pharmacologic and genetic results side by side.":["u231"],"copy|The bars compare reports, not interchangeable treatments tested against one another under identical conditions (p17–19).":["u232"],"copy|Families share more than genes: exposures, behaviors and socioeconomic circumstances can also cluster.":["u233"],"copy|The estimate that genetic factors account for 15–20% of variation in human lifespan is a population estimate, rather than the proportion of a particular person's life that is fixed in advance.":["u234"],"copy|A gene enriched among centenarians might reduce risk of one common fatal disease.":["u235"],"copy|To argue for a shared effect on aging, look for association with maintained cognition, physical capacity, sensory function or immunity, and a lower burden of multiple illnesses in addition to survival (p17–20).":["u236"],"copy|Candidate-gene studies test a biologically plausible hypothesis but can be affected by selection and nonreplication.":["u237"],"copy|Genome-wide comparisons pose a different statistical problem because of the many loci examined.":["u238"],"copy|In the chapter's account APOE was the most consistently replicated association, while FOXO3A and SIRT3 appear among candidate associations.":["u239"],"copy|Distinguish the question “Which gene was on the book's list?”":["u240"],"copy|from “Which association repeatedly appeared in genome-wide screens?”":["u241"],"copy|KITLG's absence from the particular list addresses the former and is not a universal statement about every study of human longevity (p19).":["u242"],"copy|Werner syndrome and Hutchinson–Gilford progeria illustrate why a disease that resembles aging is not a perfect model of it.":["u243"],"copy|Each reproduces selected features early while sparing or differently affecting other features of ordinary aging.":["u244"],"copy|The comparison can illuminate a molecular pathway and its tissue consequences even if it does not explain the population trajectory of aging.":["u245"],"copy|When a question calls something a progeroid phenotype, ask which manifestations match, which do not and whether the proposed comparison is molecular, clinical or demographic (p20).":["u246"],"copy|Finally, Figure 1-11 is a counterfactual calculation for a 50-year-old White woman in the United States, using historical disease-specific mortality.":["u247"],"copy|Removing mortality from a single condition leaves competing causes of death in place.":["u248"],"copy|A hypothetical slowing of several aging processes could have a larger effect, but the last bar assumes a human effect comparable to an animal dietary study; it is not an observed treatment result.":["u249"],"copy|A preventive strategy would need evidence of benefit across meaningful outcomes over an appropriate time frame as well as acceptable safety for people who are not yet ill (p20–21).":["u250"],"copy|The ethical question is not merely how long life could be prolonged.":["u251"],"copy|Consider whether additional time consists of preserved participation and independence or prolonged disability, how benefits and burdens would be distributed, and what evidence justifies marketing an intervention.":["u252"],"copy|The chapter distinguishes research on shared biological processes from sales claims for supplements or hormones.":["u253"],"copy|Plausible mechanisms, a biomarker change, animal survival and demonstrated human healthspan sit at different levels of evidence.":["u254"],"copy|Keeping those distinctions visible allows realistic discussion of prevention without turning an experimental result into a recommendation for the patient (p20–22).":["u255"],"copy|1.":["u256"],"copy|What finding supports a shared biological contribution to aging?":["u257"],"copy|2.":["u258"],"copy|How much food restriction and lifespan change does the chapter describe in rodent studies?":["u259"],"copy|3.":["u260"],"copy|What dietary pattern was the keyed answer in the older macronutrient question?":["u261"],"copy|4.":["u262"],"copy|Which essential amino acid illustrates the limitations of calorie counting?":["u263"],"copy|5.":["u264"],"copy|What did primate studies show, and where did they disagree?":["u265"],"copy|6.":["u266"],"copy|What can human dietary-restriction results support?":["u267"],"copy|7.":["u268"],"copy|How are nutrient signals linked to gene regulation?":["u269"],"copy|8.":["u270"],"copy|What does TOR help regulate?":["u271"],"copy|9.":["u272"],"copy|Which cofactor do sirtuins use?":["u273"],"copy|10.":["u274"],"copy|Why does antioxidant activity alone fail to establish an anti-aging treatment?":["u275"],"copy|11.":["u276"],"copy|How can senescence have both protective and harmful consequences?":["u277"],"copy|12.":["u278"],"copy|How much variation in human lifespan does the chapter attribute to inherited factors?":["u279"],"copy|13.":["u280"],"copy|Which answer was the exception in the older longevity-gene question?":["u281"],"copy|14.":["u282"],"copy|Which candidate was most consistently identified in the genome-wide work reviewed?":["u283"],"copy|15.":["u284"],"copy|Why do progeroid syndromes provide incomplete models of normal aging?":["u285"],"copy|Earlier sittings, chapter-level mapping — 2022-Jun Q51, 2022-Jun Q71.":["u286"],"copy|Answer":["u287","u289","u295","u297","u299","u301","u303","u305","u307","u309","u311","u313","u317","u319","u321"],"copy|An intervention delays changes across multiple tissues and late-life disorders together (p4–5).":["u288"],"copy|Approximately":["u290"],"copy|30% to 40%":["u291"],"copy|less food than freely fed controls, with a typical extension of up to":["u292"],"copy|40%":["u293"],"copy|, subject to model and regimen (p5).":["u294"],"copy|Relatively low protein compared with other macronutrients commonly produced the longest lifespan in the animal studies discussed (p6).":["u296"],"copy|Methionine. Restricted rats could consume more calories per unit of lean mass while outliving controls (p7).":["u298"],"copy|Reduced intake could delay several indices of aging or disease; studies differed on whether lifespan increased (p6).":["u300"],"copy|Improvements in measured metabolic risk factors, with extension of human lifespan without malnutrition still unresolved (p6).":["u302"],"copy|Insulin and IGF signaling influence FoxO transcription factors, connecting nutrient status to growth and stress responses (p7–8).":["u304"],"copy|Growth and protein translation, with related effects on maintenance and autophagy (p8–9).":["u306"],"copy|NAD. Different family members modify proteins and affect metabolism in different tissues (p9–10).":["u308"],"copy|Damage and stress responses interact with many pathways, and antioxidants have not reliably lengthened lifespan in the models reviewed (p10–12).":["u310"],"copy|Permanent withdrawal from division can limit tumor growth, but persistent senescent cells can alter nearby tissue through their secretions (p13–14).":["u312"],"copy|About":["u314"],"copy|15% to 20%":["u315"],"copy|; some of the variation may reflect susceptibility to particular diseases (p17–19).":["u316"],"copy|KITLG. The candidate associations cited include FOXO3A, APOE and SIRT3 (p19).":["u318"],"copy|APOE. The chapter describes replication across studies for this locus and difficulty reproducing many others (p19).":["u320"],"copy|They reproduce selected changes, while other features of normal aging are absent or differ (p20).":["u322"]}}
